#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124258 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124454 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "a01560be-9023-593a-87d3-7412f3b208a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3362" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y) = 6x^2+2y^2 - 2xy^2,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (3,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(12x-2{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4y-4xy)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,3) & =(12(3)-2(3)^{2})i+(4(3)-4(3)(3))j \\\\  & =18\\,i-24\\,j \\\\  & =⟨18,-24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the level curve that passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)=(3,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(3,3)\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨18,-24⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can leave our answer in this form. Or, if we want to leave our final answer in Cartesian form, we expand the dot product and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18(x-3)-24(y-3) & =0 \\\\ 18x-24y+18 & =0 \\\\ 3x-4y+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/102f55fe0add794d3ee0f9f044b5e35c4403d4f160fe36e97d737d33ef3abb68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124259 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f6400ab7-3684-5f0d-b8f0-1c85b0b322ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-3363" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(-3x+4)\\text{ }i-(2x-3y)\\text{ }j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(-3(1)+4)\\text{ }i-(2(1)-3(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =1\\,i-(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/01/0114bf524d085402bd27178d687a5faf176b7147c8e8726b84042b17823b6b0b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124260 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "c4ca2e8a-5a5b-54c6-983d-2f5e821084ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3364" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $6xy+3y^2 =7+ y - 3x^2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = 6xy+3y^2 - y + 3x^2 ,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y) = f_y(x,y) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x,y) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf t \\cdot \\mathbf j = 0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -6y-6x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6x(-x)+3(-x)^{2} & =7+(-x)-3{x}^{2} \\\\ -6{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =7-x-3{x}^{2} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if $x=7,$ we get $y=-7$ and the corresponding point is $(7,-7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124454 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8440b232-2d79-53cd-bad6-36fe06f0e97a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388078} {:answer/id #uuid "fec3679d-d002-508a-b44e-896ae847115f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388079} {:answer/id #uuid "d41769d3-a85e-5a1d-b0d2-621fb6f6adf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388080} {:answer/id #uuid "8d510eb4-4bca-58e9-bca7-c33fc8cc7f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388081} {:answer/id #uuid "112648f6-f22c-57a0-8ebb-0ebd53decbdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "112648f6-f22c-57a0-8ebb-0ebd53decbdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388082} :answer-field/id #uuid "b68b09e4-9252-5d68-ab26-fe3cc2a6e217" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c1bed00-c1da-539a-bd7b-b11b7bf684ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-130094" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-x+y,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-x+y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }i+(2xy+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =((-1)^{2}-1)\\,i+(2(1)(-1)+1)\\,j \\\\  & =0\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db8855f-407d-543c-b41c-01ec8863c250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+{e}^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263273} {:answer/id #uuid "310e7ac2-6457-50a9-b85b-62fe72227f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263418} {:answer/id #uuid "313c22eb-142a-5e0a-bbf7-3e694214c7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263420} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef9f166-57a7-581f-b069-feefd3a089a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263557} {:answer/id #uuid "66d4bf27-1916-5b14-ad54-71713514a6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "310e7ac2-6457-50a9-b85b-62fe72227f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263418} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0fb381c-a4a1-50c1-8a16-52f23963275a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b37d7050-f209-536a-923e-f6a34554696d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130200" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}=e-y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1} & =e-y \\\\ x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)i-{f}_{x}(1,0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+1)i-{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ t(1,0) & =(2(1)(0){e}^{0+1}+1)i-{e}^{0+1}\\,j \\\\  & =i-e\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-e\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adfa4a9b-088c-5d4c-a06a-f83cff46387a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6i + 2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122481} {:answer/id #uuid "bc43c78b-b71e-59cc-86a1-ed21b4906ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i - 6j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122482} {:answer/id #uuid "166843cc-f599-540c-a895-790beb61e4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122483} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8d92ed-bd29-5e78-ac9c-40789085eb1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6i - j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122484} {:answer/id #uuid "75ba95d3-ccdd-5335-bc47-1bc87a3a2f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i + 2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122485}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ea33b86b-0147-5727-9ec2-461d7ee2b6cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122480}] :question/id #uuid "d538882c-b1cd-5192-bbe8-86de583ea5af" :question/math-academy-id "q-36391" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^{2}y = xy + 4$. Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2, 2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b0f044-128c-5f1b-aa69-2500dbaf5ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(0,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364318} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd3ddf3-f39a-5e23-9542-809cca8045dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364319} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3c3702-cba3-57ad-b3e0-2c994913bd41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(0,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364320} {:answer/id #uuid "71ef2f17-55ba-507e-8bbc-fc7b6bfebfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364321} {:answer/id #uuid "9e254c1d-c4f8-51bc-9734-35d90904ed56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdd3ddf3-f39a-5e23-9542-809cca8045dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364319} :answer-field/id #uuid "e462412c-3cbc-5541-9147-125fc8a64b29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32727d15-71dd-5c57-93a9-b5f15381c7c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72416" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2xy-3{x}^{2}=-y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3{x}^{2}=-y \\\\ 2xy-3{x}^{2}+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy-3{x}^{2}+y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-3{x}^{2}+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-3{x}^{2}+y)j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i-2(y-3x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((2x+1)\\,i-2(y-3x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -2(y-3x) & =0 \\\\ y & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=3x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3{x}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ 2x(3x)-3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=0$, we get $y=0$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-3$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-3).$"}]}
